WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.106
Human error is a normal feature of complex work.

00:00:03.106 --> 00:00:11.323
Even skilled, careful people miss steps, misread signals, choose based on stale assumptions, or act too quickly under pressure.

00:00:11.323 --> 00:00:15.658
A strong performance system does not pretend errors will disappear.

00:00:15.658 --> 00:00:22.452
It designs work so mistakes are less likely, easier to catch, and less likely to become serious failures.

00:00:22.452 --> 00:00:27.240
Checklists and standard operating procedures support repeatable execution.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.281
They do not replace expertise; they protect it.

00:00:30.281 --> 00:00:37.852
A good checklist helps ensure that known critical steps are not missed when attention is limited or pressure is high.

00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:44.581
Procedures also make expectations visible so teams can improve the process instead of relying on memory.

00:00:44.581 --> 00:00:49.304
Pre-mortems, decision journals, and peer review improve decision quality.

00:00:49.304 --> 00:00:53.122
A pre-mortem asks what could go wrong before action begins.

00:00:53.122 --> 00:00:59.981
A decision journal captures the reasoning, assumptions, and expected outcomes at the time of the decision.

00:00:59.981 --> 00:01:06.451
Peer review adds another perspective that may catch blind spots, unclear logic, or overlooked risks.

00:01:06.451 --> 00:01:09.427
High-risk actions deserve deliberate friction.

00:01:09.427 --> 00:01:12.469
Slow down when the cost of being wrong is high.

00:01:12.469 --> 00:01:14.604
Separate planning from execution.

00:01:14.604 --> 00:01:17.192
Confirm scope before irreversible steps.

00:01:17.192 --> 00:01:19.780
Use two-person review where appropriate.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.274
Design systems that catch mistakes before they matter.

00:01:23.274 --> 00:01:28.515
Reliability comes from respecting human limits and building controls around them.

00:01:28.515 --> 00:01:35.180
Error management works best when the team can discuss mistakes without turning every review into blame.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:42.879
The useful question is not only who made the error, but what made the error likely, hard to detect, or easy to amplify.

00:01:42.879 --> 00:01:49.156
That shift helps teams improve the system while still preserving accountability for careful work.
